# src/test/regress/yb_merge_scan_schedule
#
# Test pggate merge sorting of IN queries that are not part of the sort order.
#
# The main structure of the tests in this schedule is to compare running a
# query with merge scan on and off.  The explain plan should have "Merge ..."
# fields under index scan nodes to indicate that.  Keep in mind that some
# negative tests expect merge scan to NOT be used: those should only explain
# analyze and not run the queries because there's no point in running the
# queries.
#
# SELECT targets generally follow a pattern of this order:
# 1. Columns equal to constant
# 1. Columns part of sorting, where non-key column "n" is always the last sort
#    column (except for cases where it is absolutely not needed such as
#    DISTINCT and GROUP BY) in order to provide consistently ordered results
# 1. Columns part of SAOP
# 1. Optionally, other columns
#
# Statistics matter to these tests because the plan a query gets depends on
# them.  Aim for the statistics a real cluster would have.  These tests run
# with the cost based optimizer on, and a cluster running it also runs the auto
# analyze service, which analyzes a table for you soon after the table changes.
# For realistic plans and plan stability, disable auto analyze and run ANALYZE
# manually instead:
# 1. Run ANALYZE once a table is created and filled.
# 1. Run ANALYZE on the table again after creating an index on an expression,
#    such as ((a + b)).  Such an index gets statistics of its own, describing
#    the values the expression produces, and only ANALYZE collects them.
#    Creating the index does not.
#
# This works because these tables are small.  ANALYZE looks at a sample of
# 300 times default_statistics_target rows, which is 30000 by default, so a
# table that fits in the sample has every one of its rows looked at and gets
# the same statistics every time.  A bigger table gets a random subset of its
# rows instead, and then its statistics differ from run to run, which moves
# plans and breaks tests.  Keep tables here well under that size.  #34028 asks
# for a way to make ANALYZE deterministic whatever the table size.
#
# Temporary tables are the exception.  The auto analyze service connects as its
# own session, and one session cannot see another session's temporary tables,
# so a real cluster leaves them unanalyzed unless the session analyzes them
# itself.  Leave them unanalyzed here as well.

# Setup is used by tests in later lines.
test: yb.orig.merge_scan_setup

# This test reads r5n.
test: yb.orig.merge_scan_temp

# These tests each touch different tables.
test: yb.orig.merge_scan_range
test: yb.orig.merge_scan_hash
test: yb.orig.merge_scan_partition
test: yb.orig.merge_scan_colocated
test: yb.orig.merge_scan_relabel
test: yb.orig.merge_scan_cross_type

# This test uses tv_tbl like the relabel test on an earlier line, adding an
# index that it tears down.
test: yb.orig.merge_scan_collation

# This test uses bkt_tbl, adding an index and RLS state that it tears down for
# tests in later lines.
test: yb.orig.merge_scan_rls

# These tests touch multiple tables.
test: yb.orig.merge_scan_join
test: yb.orig.merge_scan_derive

# This test defines its own tables.
test: yb.orig.merge_scan_degenerate
test: yb.orig.merge_scan_plan_cache
